  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Find where the tangent to the curve $y={x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ at the point  $(-1,-2)$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nThe tangent intersects the $y$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)={x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-(-2{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =3(-1)^{2}+\\frac{2}{(-1)^{3}} \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =1\\cdot [x-(-1)] \\\\ y+2 & =x+1 \\\\ y & =x+1-2 \\\\ y & =x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0-1 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,-1).$",
